      q: 'An ionic crystal like NaCl is brittle because:',
      choices: [
        'Its bonds are the weakest of the four types',
        'Sliding the planes by one ion spacing brings like charges face to face, and the electrostatic repulsion cracks the crystal',
        'Ions cannot vibrate',
        'It has no defects',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Ionic bonds are actually strong — the problem is geometry. A dislocation step that would be harmless in copper shoves + charges against + charges. Charge + geometry, not weakness, is why salts and ceramics shatter instead of denting.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Alloying copper with tin to make bronze improves the alloy mainly by:',
      choices: [
        'Changing the electron sea into covalent bonds',
        'Melting the tin',
        'Adding strain fields around differently-sized atoms that pin dislocations — the metal is harder to deform',
        'Removing grain boundaries',
      ],
      answer: 2,
      why: 'Tin atoms distort the copper lattice locally; dislocations must drag through these obstacles. Solid-solution strengthening plus the dislocation-pinning it promotes is why bronze tools outcut copper ones around 3300 bce — materials science operating three millennia before the mechanism was understood.',
    },
  ],
